#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# parametric_n_dsolve_value

#% text
# `parametric_n_dsolve_value(eqs, expr, [t, t0, t1], [p1, p2, ...])` gives a function `f` of the parameters `p1`, `p2`, ...; `f(v1, v2, ...)` is the value of `expr` along the numerical solution of the differential equations `eqs` with the parameters set to `v1`, `v2`, ....

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The equations are prepared once; each call `f(v1, v2, ...)` only integrates them numerically. This makes many calls cheap, as in fitting, scanning and sampling.

#% item
# When `expr` is the value at a fixed time, such as `y(t1)`, a call gives a number. When it depends on `t`, such as `y(t)`, a call gives a function of `t`.

#% item
# The parameters may appear in the equations, in the initial values and in the options.

#% item
# A call with a symbolic argument stays unevaluated, so `f` can appear inside expressions that are evaluated later at numbers.

#% item
# The options are those of `n_dsolve`, such as `method`, `digits`, `precision_goal` and `accuracy_goal`.

#% item
# The number of arguments of a call must equal the number of parameters.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The value y(1) for y' = s - k y, y(0) = 0, as a function of s and k:

#% cell
f = parametric_n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == s - k*y(t), y(0) == 0], y(1), [t, 0, 1], [s, k])

#% cell
f(2.0, 1.0)
#% output
#= 1.2642411163145382

#% text
# The exact value is 2(1 - e^-1):

#% cell
n(2*(1 - exp(-1)))
#% output
#= 1.2642411176571153

#% text
# A scan over k:

#% cell
table(f(2.0, k), k, range(0.5, 3, 0.5))
#% output
#= [1.5738773605383278, 1.2642411163145382, 1.0358264515298576, 0.8646647149518953, 0.7343319992884335, 0.6334752859140269]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A parameter in the initial value, and the whole solution as a function of t:

#% cell
g = parametric_n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == -k*y(t), y(0) == y0], y(t), [t, 0, 2], [k, y0]); g(1.0, 3.0)(0.5)
#% output
#= 1.8195919754535632

#% text
# A second-order equation, the oscillator x'' = -w^2 x, observed at t = pi:

#% cell
p = parametric_n_dsolve_value([x''(t) == -w^2*x(t), x(0) == 1, x'(0) == 0], x(pi), [t, 0, pi], [w]); map(w => p(w), [0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0])
#% output
#= [-9.8055333297431e-10, -0.999999991005721, 2.0865214278575905e-9, 0.9999999846528167]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# More digits:

#% cell
h = parametric_n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == -k*y(t), y(0) == 1], y(1), [t, 0, 1], [k], digits: 20); h(1)
#% output
#= 0.36787944117144232160

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A chi-square measure of how well a parameter pair fits a measurement y(1) = 2 with error 0.1, as in a Bayesian fit by Markov chain Monte Carlo:

#% cell
fn chi_square(th) = ((f(th[1], th[2]) - 2)/0.1)^2; [chi_square([2.0, 1.0]), chi_square([3.0, 1.0])]
#% output
#= [54.134113492207696, 1.0740902417694214]

#% text
# The value c of k for which y(1) = 1 when s = 2:

#% cell
find_root(f(2.0, c) == 1, [c, 1])
#% output
#= [c -> 1.5936242555697622]

#% text
# The value u of s that fits the measurement best when k = 1:

#% cell
find_minimum(((f(u, 1.0) - 2)/0.1)^2, [u, 1])
#% output
#= [1.7718618123918444e-20, [u -> 3.1639534162812275]]

#% text
# The result as a curve of the parameter:

#% cell
list_plot(table([k, f(2.0, k)], k, range(0.1, 3, 0.1)), joined: true, x_label: "k", y_label: "y(1)")
#% output
#= image("parametric_n_dsolve_value.files/parametric_n_dsolve_value-11.svgz")

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Each call gives the same value as `n_dsolve_value` with the parameters put in:

#% cell
f(2.0, 1.0) - n_dsolve_value([y'(t) == 2.0 - 1.0*y(t), y(0) == 0], y(1), [t, 0, 1])
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The number of values must match the number of parameters:

#% cell
f(2.0, 1.0, 3.0)
#% output
#! failure(argument, parametric_n_dsolve_value, "2 parameter values expected")

#% text
# A variable with the same name as a parameter of the function cannot be replaced by a value, so a search in it fails; use another name, as above:

#% cell
f(2.0, k) /. k -> 1.0
#% output
#= 1.2642411163145382

#% cell
find_root(f(2.0, k) == 1, [k, 1])
#% output
#= [k -> 1.5936242555697622]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n_dsolve_value`, `n_dsolve`, `parametric_function`, `find_root`, `find_minimum`
